      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "",
        "copy": "<p>Skin laxity is one of the most common concerns I hear, from the face to the arms, abdomen, and flanks. As a plastic surgeon, a significant portion of what I do is about tightening. And for years, I have watched aesthetic technologies overpromise and underdeliver in this space, leaving the most meaningful interventions to the operating room.</p><p><em><strong>OndaPro is different. I do not say that lightly</strong></em></p>"
      }
    },
    "divider_BHLrMx": {
      "type": "divider",
      "name": "Divider",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "insight_copy_section_xMkWci": {
      "type": "insight-copy-section",
      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "The Science",
        "copy": "<p>OndaPro uses microwave energy at 2.45 GHz, a frequency that selectively targets the dermis and subcutaneous fat with a level of depth precision that radio frequency and ultrasound-based devices cannot reliably achieve.</p><p>At the tissue level, two distinct processes occur simultaneously.</p><p>The first is an immediate thermal effect. Controlled collagen denaturation causes the existing collagen network to contract, producing visible tightening from the first session.</p><p>The second is a regenerative cascade. Thermal injury triggers a wound-healing response, upregulating heat shock proteins involved in collagen synthesis and, critically, downregulating matrix metalloproteinases, the enzymes responsible for collagen breakdown.</p><p>This second point matters. Most technologies stimulate new collagen production. Fewer also reduce collagen degradation. OndaPro does both, which translates to net collagen gain rather than simple turnover.</p>"
      }
    },
